        "ContentType": "Description",
        "SortOrder": 0,
        "Content": "Laurel Hurst, Model Farm Road, Cork, T12 D6KV\n” A rare opportunity to acquire an established detached residence on approximately 0.94 acres on Model Farm Road”\n\nOM2 O'Mahony are delighted to present Laurel Hurst, an exceptionally interesting residential property occupying a substantial site of approximately 0.94 acres in one of Cork City's most established and sought-after residential locations. Positioned directly on the Model Farm Road, the property combines the charm of an existing detached bungalow with an unusually generous landholding, mature gardens and extensive grounds, all within convenient reach of Cork City, Ballincollig and the western suburbs.\nThe scale, configuration and location of the property are undoubtedly among its most compelling attributes. The site is generally level and predominantly rectangular in form, extending considerably to the rear of the existing residence. Laurel Hurst therefore represents a rare opportunity to acquire a substantial private holding along the Model Farm Road, suitable for continued use as a distinctive family residence while also offering considerable scope for a purchaser to explore the property's longer-term possibilities, subject to planning permission and all relevant statutory requirements.\n\nLocation \nLaurel Hurst enjoys an exceptional position along Model Farm Road, one of Cork City's most established and sought-after residential addresses. Despite its mature, almost countryside setting, the property is positioned within the western environs of Cork City, offering an unusual combination of privacy, space and accessibility.\nThe location places a wide range of Cork City's major educational, medical and employment centres within easy reach, including Cork University Hospital, University College Cork, Munster Technological University, Wilton and Bishopstown, together with the city centre itself. Model Farm Road is also home to a number of major employers and established residential neighbourhoods, while an excellent range of schools, shops, sporting facilities and everyday amenities are available throughout the surrounding area.\nConnectivity is another considerable advantage, with convenient access to the N22 and N40, providing straightforward connections across Cork City and to the wider regional and national road network. For a property of this scale, Laurel Hurst occupies a particularly interesting position, offering the space, greenery and established grounds more commonly associated with a rural property while remaining remarkably convenient to Cork City.\nLands & Gardens\nLaurel Hurst stands on a substantial holding extending to approximately 0.94 acres, with the residence positioned towards the Model Farm Road frontage and the grounds extending extensively to the rear.\nThe property is approached via its own driveway, while a separate wrought-iron pedestrian gate opens to a pathway leading through the front garden to the residence. Traditional boundary walls, mature hedging and established planting provide a pleasant sense of enclosure from the roadside.\nThe front and side gardens have been carefully established over many years and incorporate lawns, mature trees, hedging and an abundance of flowering plants and shrubs, including an attractive Japanese maple. A patio adjoining the side of the residence is directly accessible from the kitchen and provides a sheltered outdoor space overlooking the gardens.\nBeyond the immediate gardens, the holding opens into an extensive rear lawn and garden area, one of the property's most distinctive features. Mature trees and established boundaries provide considerable greenery and privacy, while sections of traditional sod-and-stone and original stone walling add further character.\nThe property's depth is particularly apparent from the aerial photography, with the grounds extending considerably beyond the residence and creating an increasingly open aspect towards the rear. The result is a remarkably spacious setting given the property's proximity to Cork City.\nA substantial detached garage/shed, measuring approximately c. 15m x 4m, provides useful ancillary space, while a number of additional detached timber sheds are positioned throughout the grounds.\n\n\nDetached Bungalow Residence\nAt the front of the holding stands the existing detached single-storey residence, extending to approximately 62.4 sq.m. (672 sq.ft.). The property provides compact and versatile accommodation with considerable traditional character and presents an excellent opportunity for refurbishment, extension or reconfiguration to create a home tailored to a purchaser's individual requirements, subject to any necessary permissions.\nThe entrance porch leads directly into the principal sitting room, an attractive central room featuring timber flooring, a feature fireplace, pine-clad ceiling and windows providing natural light from both aspects.\nThe accommodation can presently be configured as three bedrooms with two living areas, or alternatively four bedrooms, depending on requirements. The rooms retain a simple traditional character, with high ceilings and a mixture of timber and contemporary flooring.\nTo the rear, the kitchen/dining room is a particularly bright space overlooking the gardens. It incorporates fitted cabinetry, tiled flooring and a solid-fuel stove, while glazed doors open directly onto the adjoining patio and garden, creating an excellent connection between the house and its mature outdoor surroundings.\nA further living room is positioned beyond the kitchen and overlooks the rear grounds, providing flexibility as an additional reception room, home office or fourth bedroom.\nThe bathroom has been comprehensively modernised with contemporary floor and wall tiling and incorporates a generous walk-in shower, WC, wash-hand basin, heated towel radiator and underfloor heating.\nWhile perfectly capable of continued residential use, the existing bungalow also offers a purchaser the opportunity to reimagine the residence within its substantial mature site, whether through refurbishment, extension or a more comprehensive redevelopment proposal, subject to planning permission.\n\n\nPotential\nA particularly compelling aspect of Laurel Hurst is the combination of its approximately 0.94-acre landholding, established residential use and strategic Model Farm Road location.\nThe property is situated within lands currently designated ZO 20 City Hinterland under the Cork City Development Plan 20222028. The primary objective of this zoning is to protect and improve rural amenity and provide for the development of agriculture, while maintaining the distinction between urban areas and the surrounding countryside. The zoning also provides for consideration of certain other uses, subject to the relevant planning objectives and requirements. \nWith the existing residence concentrated towards the Model Farm Road frontage, the property benefits from a substantial depth of ground extending to the rear. This creates ample space from which a purchaser may explore the potential for extension, redevelopment or other future opportunities, subject in all cases to planning permission, the ZO 20 zoning objectives and all relevant statutory requirements.\nThe property's scale and configuration, combined with its proximity to Cork City, make it an especially interesting proposition for purchasers seeking not simply an existing home, but a substantial residential holding with longer-term potential.\nProspective purchasers should satisfy themselves independently as to the suitability of the property for any proposed development or alternative use.\n\n\nAdditional details:\n-Mains Water\n-Septic Tank \n-Solid Fuel Central Heating\n-BER: G\n-Standard block construction and slate roof\n\nDisclaimer: These particulars are issued by OM2 O'Mahony on the understanding that all negotiations are conducted through this office. While every care has been taken in preparing the particulars, they are for guidance only and do not form part of any contract. Descriptions, dimensions, distances, references to condition and permissions are given in good faith but are not to be relied upon as statements or representations of fact. Purchasers should satisfy themselves by inspection or otherwise as to the correctness of each statement. The vendor does not make or give, and neither OM2 O'Mahony nor any of its employees has authority to make or give, any representation or warranty in relation to the property.\n"
